A coworker gives you one line to print a collection of titles.
Their Bash prompt accepts it. Your Fish prompt points at the opening
parenthesis and objects. The terminal window didn't become defective
on the walk between desks. The line is written in a different shell
language. You can keep the prompt you like and still run a script
written for another interpreter.

\begin{objectives}
\item Identify the difference between the shell at your prompt and the interpreter running a script.
\item Pursue a portable recipe without guessing from the operating system or prompt shape.
\item Recognize useful differences among POSIX sh, Bash, Zsh, Fish, Ksh and the C-shell family.
\end{objectives}

\section{Identify the thing you want to change}
\label{sec:29-identity}
\index{shell families}
\index{login shell}

There are at least three questions behind ``What shell do I have?''
Which interpreter reads lines at this prompt? Which shell was
configured for this account at login? Which language does a
particular file ask to use? The answers can be different. For
instance, you can open a Fish prompt, choose to start a temporary
Zsh session inside it, and run a POSIX shell script from that
session. None of that changes the account's login setting.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-29/roles.tikz}
\caption{A chosen interpreter can differ from the interactive shell.}
\label{fig:29-roles}
\VisualDescription{The terminal window leads to a Fish prompt. The prompt invokes sh, which reads a script file in the POSIX shell language.}
\end{figure}

Check for interpreters by name from a fresh \texttt{sh} session.
Type \texttt{sh} first. Then type the following lines separately;
these queries neither install nor launch the optional shells:

\begin{terminal}
command -v sh
command -v bash
command -v zsh
command -v fish
command -v ksh
command -v tcsh
\end{terminal}

As in Chapter~\ref{ch:28}, the \texttt{-v} option of
\texttt{command} reports what the shell would use. An absent
optional shell can produce no path and a nonzero status; it is
fine to skip its experiments. A warning sign is a name resolved
to a local function or wrapper you don't recognize rather than
the interpreter you intended. Check its origin before running
it. Nothing below requires installing anything to follow the
explanation.

\begin{terminal}
printf 'Session clue: <%s>\n' "$0"
printf 'Account clue: <%s>\n' "${SHELL-unset}"
\end{terminal}

\texttt{\$0} can show the name used to start this shell,
usually \texttt{sh} in this exercise. When a shell runs a
script, it may instead give the script name.
\texttt{SHELL} is usually an inherited preference or login
shell path. It can be absent, stale, or deliberately set to
something else. Treat both as clues, not as conclusive probes.
Knowing which interpreter you explicitly requested is better
than trying to infer it from a customized prompt.

\section{Portable is a language choice, not an operating-system label}
\label{sec:29-posix}
\index{POSIX shell}
\index{sh@\texttt{sh}}

The POSIX shell specification describes a language shared by
multiple implementations. It is not a program with a single
version number. On different Linux installations, the name
\texttt{sh} can select Dash, Bash, BusyBox Ash or another
implementation. macOS has long supplied a system \texttt{sh}
alongside Bash and Zsh; on a recent stock setup the usual
interactive default for new accounts is Zsh. FreeBSD and
NetBSD include their own \texttt{sh}; OpenBSD's \texttt{sh}
comes from its Korn shell family. Those facts help explain
why a test on one system is not a test of all of them.
Account defaults can be changed locally. Check your own
manuals and actual command lookup before making a claim
about your computer.

\begin{terminal}
sh -c 'set -- "Paper notes" "Photo index"; printf "<%s>\n" "$@"'
\end{terminal}

\texttt{-c} runs the fixed source string that follows. The
parent's single quotes preserve the dollar sign for the new
shell, which assigns the two positional parameters with
\texttt{set --} and prints them with \cmd|"$@"|. Expect
\texttt{<Paper notes>} and \texttt{<Photo index>}. This is a
small portable way to keep a short group of arguments
without invoking an interpreter's array feature. If you
have Bash or Zsh, you can also try the same code with
\texttt{bash -c} or \texttt{zsh -c} replacing \texttt{sh -c}.
Passing this test is not proof that either native mode
implements every POSIX default; it is one shared idiom.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.05in}YY}
\toprule
Family & Why use it & What not to assume \\
\midrule
POSIX sh & Share short, conservative scripts across many Unix-like installations. & The underlying implementation is the same everywhere. \\
Bash & Rich scripting features and an extensive ecosystem, with a familiar interactive style. & Bash-only syntax works in Dash or every system's sh. \\
Zsh & Friendly interactive facilities such as completion and extensive glob controls. & Native glob and splitting rules are Bash defaults. \\
Fish & Built-in interactive suggestions and completion, and a distinct language and list model. & A POSIX assignment is native Fish syntax. \\
Ksh & Korn shell scripting, arithmetic and other extensions for an existing Ksh workflow. & Two systems' Korn variants support exactly the same extensions. \\
Csh/Tcsh & Maintain an established C-shell workflow; Tcsh adds interactive editing and completion. & The assignment and loop syntax is that of sh. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{dialoguebox}{Whose shell reads it?}
\Brian{If I'm using Fish, does that make every script I run a Fish script?}
\Stephen{No. If you pass a file to \texttt{sh}, that program reads the file. Fish just starts \texttt{sh}.}
\Brian{Then why did my attempt to paste a Bash line at this prompt fail?}
\Stephen{You didn't hand a file to Bash that time. The shell at the prompt tried to read the line itself.}
\Brian{So I can keep the helpful suggestions at the prompt and still share a script with a friend on BSD?}
\Stephen{Yes. Give the script a clearly documented language and test it with an interpreter on the intended system.}
\DialogueConclusion{The reader of the source sets the rules.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Compare a few intentional differences}
\label{sec:29-dialects}
\index{Bash}
\index{Zsh}
\index{Fish}
\index{Korn shell}
\index{C shell}

Bash and Zsh both have named arrays, in which one name
refers to a list of values. Fish treats its variables as
lists, with ordinary scalar-looking values being lists
with one element. The following demonstrations are
separate commands at the practice \texttt{sh} prompt.
Skip the line for any interpreter absent on your computer.
The fixed strings after \texttt{-c} are examples of the
target language, protected by the parent shell's single
quotes. Do not substitute source strings from untrusted
files or messages.

\begin{terminal}
bash -c 'title=("Paper notes" "Photo index"); printf "<%s>\n" "${title[0]}"'
zsh -f -c 'title=("Paper notes" "Photo index"); printf "<%s>\n" "${title[1]}"'
fish --no-config -c 'set title "Paper notes" "Photo index"; printf "<%s>\n" $title[1]'
\end{terminal}

Each example prints \texttt{<Paper notes>}. In those native
default configurations the three spellings select the first
element by Bash index zero, Zsh index one and Fish index one,
respectively. Zsh's \texttt{-f} suppresses most startup-file
processing, though the global \texttt{zshenv} still runs;
site configuration can change native defaults. Fish's
\texttt{--no-config} asks it not to read configuration files.
Bash's noninteractive startup may use a file indicated by
\texttt{BASH\_ENV}. These are not perfectly fresh simulations
of every account. If your result differs, check the installed
version and startup behavior before changing your files.

The example with Fish doesn't need double quotes around the
single list element to keep its internal space. Do not copy
that convention to an unquoted POSIX variable expansion.
Conversely, dropping a POSIX \texttt{name=value} onto its
own line in a Fish script is not the way Fish assigns a
variable: use its \texttt{set} command. A familiar utility
like \texttt{printf} can still put the result on standard
output. The difference here is the shell language that
prepares those arguments, not the ability to display text.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-29/first.tikz}
\caption{The first element has different indexes in the three native modes.}
\label{fig:29-first}
\VisualDescription{Bash at index zero, Zsh at index one and Fish at index one all point to the first element, Paper notes.}
\end{figure}

Korn shells include multiple variants, notably ksh93,
mksh and the OpenBSD Korn shell. \texttt{ksh} names
whichever implementation is installed under that name,
if any. For this exercise, the POSIX list idiom above
is suitable for Ksh. Unlike the named-array comparison,
that idiom does not rely on a Ksh extension.
For a new Ksh-specific script, check whether the options
or syntax you need are in the local implementation;
do not infer that all Korn shells are interchangeable.

\begin{terminal}
ksh -c 'set -- "Paper notes" "Photo index"; printf "<%s>\n" "$1"'
\end{terminal}

The expected result is \texttt{<Paper notes>} (manual-based
example). The C-shell family, whose syntax was influenced by
C, is a separate language. Tcsh extends Csh; neither is
selected by invoking ordinary \texttt{sh}. To see a different
variable assignment and lookup, use this optional example
if Tcsh is installed:

\begin{terminal}
tcsh -f -c 'set tag = "Paper notes"; echo "$tag"'
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{Paper notes} (manual-based example).
\texttt{-f} tells Tcsh not to read startup files;
\texttt{-c} takes the command string. The separate words
around \texttt{=} are deliberate here, in contrast with
POSIX shell assignment in Chapter~\ref{ch:27}. This example
uses \texttt{echo} with an ordinary string containing no
ambiguous option or backslash. A reader maintaining an
existing C-shell script should use the appropriate manual,
not convert a few punctuation marks by intuition.

\begin{quicktable}{YY}
\toprule
If the task is\ldots & Fit the interpreter to it \\
\midrule
Share a small script with users of several systems & Start with conservative POSIX syntax and test a second implementation. \\
Use well-tested features of one interpreter & Document the required interpreter and version, and show an explicit invocation. \\
Try a different interactive experience & Start the optional shell for a temporary session; exit to return. \\
Understand a historical or inherited script & Read its invocation instructions and local shell documentation before modifying it. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Run the file in its own language}
\label{sec:29-invocation}

The common first line \texttt{\#!/bin/sh} on a script is
called a \emph{shebang}. On the systems discussed here,
when you directly invoke an executable script, that line
can identify the interpreter by path. But in
\texttt{sh script.sh}, \texttt{sh} is already selected;
it opens the script as input. Running \texttt{sh} on a
Bash-specific script doesn't honor a Bash shebang by handing
the file over to Bash. And a filename ending in
\texttt{.sh}, \texttt{.bash} or \texttt{.fish} is a helpful
convention, not instructions to the operating system.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-29/select.tikz}
\caption{Two invocation methods select an interpreter at different points.}
\label{fig:29-select}
\VisualDescription{With an explicit sh invocation, sh reads the file. With direct execution of an executable script, the operating system uses the shebang interpreter path.}
\end{figure}

This chapter needs no new script or startup file. To try
another interpreter interactively, type its name only if
your availability check found the expected installation.
For example, run \texttt{zsh -f}, then try the following
two lines separately and return using \texttt{exit}.
Do not type \texttt{exit} if the shell didn't start.

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' "Different prompt, same terminal"
exit
\end{terminal}

That temporary session is a safe comparison. There is no
need to change your login shell to make a script work.
Before using syntax you just learned at a different prompt,
ask whether you are comparing the commands' arguments, the
interactive amenities, or the script language. Each can
change without changing the other two.

\section{Decide what to put in the handoff}
\label{sec:29-handoff}

Imagine sending this instruction with a small script:
``Run it at the terminal.'' That tells the recipient where
to sit, not which program reads the source. A more useful
instruction might say ``Plain-text POSIX shell script;
run it as \texttt{sh report.sh} from the folder containing
the inputs.'' That also makes explicit which working
directory the recipe assumes. If you rely on Bash arrays,
say so and use \texttt{bash report.bash} instead. The named
interpreter is as much a part of the recipe as the name of
the script.

For a small script, choose a known input that shows
a meaningful boundary. Here both arguments have spaces:

\begin{terminal}
sh -c 'set -- "Paper notes" "Photo index"; printf "%s\n" "$#"'
sh -c 'set -- "Paper notes" "Photo index"; printf "<%s>\n" "$2"'
\end{terminal}

Your baseline is \texttt{2} and \texttt{<Photo index>}.
Someone using a different implementation can check a
portable recipe with the same input. If you are comparing
native shells instead, don't hide a language change
behind the same script label. Identify the feature used
in each version and compare what the versions accomplish
with identical inputs, not which spelling is shortest.
An array offers named structured storage where the short
portable list above reuses positional arguments; neither
is a drop-in substitute for the other in a longer script.

Be careful also with syntax checking. \texttt{sh -n file}
asks this \texttt{sh} to read the file without executing
it. A successful result means this particular interpreter
could read the syntax, not that a different interpreter
can read it, or that any of them will handle all the inputs
correctly. For behavior, run the intended interpreter on
invented input. For portability, run another implementation
and record which one. If you cannot, name the specification
or manuals you consulted and leave the result marked
untested on the other system.

There is also a legitimate reason not to aim for portability.
If a script's only job is adjusting how the Fish prompt
displays a folder, it belongs to that interactive setup.
Rewriting it as POSIX shell to satisfy an unrelated recipe
would obscure what it does. At the other end of the scale,
a recipe that wraps a third-party utility cannot be portable
merely because its syntax is POSIX. The recipient needs the
same utility or a documented alternative, too. Separate
those two kinds of dependency: first who reads the source,
then which commands that source asks the reader to run.

\section{Exercises and cleanup}

\begin{exercise}{Probe the intended interpreter}
\label{ex:29-available}
Check whether your system supplies \texttt{sh} and
\texttt{ksh}. Explain what you know from the output
without guessing the underlying implementation.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
command -v sh
command -v ksh
\end{terminal}
The first should identify the practice interpreter; the
second may be absent. A path or command name is not a
version report. These queries create no files.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Share the data, not the array syntax}
\label{ex:29-posix}
Run a POSIX command string that stores \texttt{Saturday
group} and \texttt{Sunday group} as separate arguments.
Print just the second one without using an array.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
sh -c 'set -- "Saturday group" "Sunday group"; printf "<%s>\n" "$2"'
\end{terminal}
Expect \texttt{<Sunday group>}. The child shell exits and
its positional arguments are discarded. No file is made.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Choose an index on purpose}
\label{ex:29-index}
If Bash or Zsh is available, rerun its array example with
the index for \texttt{Photo index}. If neither is available,
predict the output and verify the same title using POSIX
positional parameters instead.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
bash -c 'title=("Paper notes" "Photo index"); printf "<%s>\n" "${title[1]}"'
zsh -f -c 'title=("Paper notes" "Photo index"); printf "<%s>\n" "${title[2]}"'
sh -c 'set -- "Paper notes" "Photo index"; printf "<%s>\n" "$2"'
\end{terminal}
Run only the available variants. Each should show
\texttt{<Photo index>}. All practice lists disappear when
their child shells exit.
\end{solution}

Leave the outer practice \texttt{sh} with \texttt{exit} once
you have finished. Nothing in this exercise requires changing
your account or cleaning any directories.

\begin{takeaways}
\item Your prompt, your account's default and a script's interpreter are separate choices.
\item Check a shell's name and documentation before copying its extensions to another language.
\item A conservative POSIX idiom is often the easiest way to share a small task.
\end{takeaways}

